om these vaccines  will last is one thing that's still  being studied with the original coveted  vaccines some studies show that  neutralizing antibodies which help  prevent viruses from infecting cells may  start to wane after six months or even  sooner with newer variants but vaccines  also induce T cells which attack cells  infected by the virus when antibodies  fail some studies show that these cells  might offer longer protection even after  antibodies weighing  some experts think annual shots are the  best way to balance timing others aren't  convinced yet  there are experts there are doctors out  there who say hold on maybe we need to  do it more frequently maybe we need to  have a new vaccine every time we see a  sort of a worrisome variant we have some  who are saying one booster shot will  last you for several years maybe just  the first booster will be enough for  some people some like Dr offit also say  that while it's not clear if the entire  population would benefit from updated  boosters there's a clear benefit to some  this booster dosing frankly for for  those who are at highest risk is really  about the elderly people who are uh who  compromised people who have long-term  heart or belong  neurological disease but I don't think  there's there's a downside to taking it  I just don't think the upside is quite  what it's made out today  Offutt was in a minority that voted  against including an Omicron component  to updated boosters in June he says he  would have liked to see more data on the  vaccine and how it was administered I  didn't think data were presented that  we're convincing that the bivalent  vaccine was any better than the the  current vaccine but other experts say  that updated boosters will be a useful  tool in fighting the virus moving  forward modeling projections show that  an uptake of updated covid-19 vaccine  doses similar to an annual flu vaccine  coverage early this fall could prevent  as many as 100  000 hospitalizations 9  000 